---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import DevTimeEstimator from '../../components/DevTimeEstimator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Development Time Estimator" description="Pick common features to build a rough project time estimate, with a buffer for the unknowns.">
  <DevTimeEstimator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why the buffer matters</h2>
  <p>Software estimates are notoriously optimistic — a 20-30% buffer for integration issues, edge cases, and scope creep is standard practice, not padding.</p>

  <h2>Common Estimation Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Estimating only the "happy path" implementation time</strong>, ignoring edge cases, error handling, and testing that typically take as long as the core feature itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Presenting an estimate without a buffer</strong> as if it were a guaranteed deadline, setting unrealistic expectations from the start.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Dev Time Estimator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Building a rough project timeline before committing to a client deadline</li>
    <li>Communicating realistic expectations to stakeholders about feature delivery</li>
    <li>Comparing scope options by their estimated time impact</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Dev Time Estimate FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How accurate are these estimates?</strong> They're a rough starting point based on commonly cited effort for each feature type — actual time varies significantly by codebase complexity, team experience, and unknowns that only surface once you start building.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I share the raw estimate with a client, or the buffered one?</strong> Share the buffered estimate — presenting only the optimistic number sets an expectation that's unlikely to hold once integration issues and edge cases surface.</p>
</ToolLayout>
